artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ be symmetric and let $X_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times b}$ be a starting block with orthonormal columns. The block Lanczos algorithm generates, for $k=1,2,\ldots$, blocks $X_k \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times b}$ and $b\times b$ matrices $M_k$ (symmetric) and $B_k$ (upper triangular) via the three-term recurrence
$$ A X_k = X_{k-1} B_{k-1}^T + X_k M_k + X_{k+1} B_k, $$
where $M_k = X_k^T A X_k$ and $X_{k+1} B_k$ is obtained by orthogonalizing $A X_k - X_{k-1} B_{k-1}^T - X_k M_k$ against $X_k$ (with $X_0 := 0$, $B_0 := 0$) and then performing an intra-block QR-type orthogonalization to produce $X_{k+1}$ with orthonormal columns and the upper-triangular factor $B_k$. After $k$ steps, the computed block tridiagonal matrix
$$ T_k = \begin{pmatrix} M_1 & B_1^T & & \\ B_1 & M_2 & B_2^T & \\ & B_2 & \ddots & \ddots \\ & & \ddots & M_k \end{pmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{kb \times kb} $$
yields approximate eigenvalues (Ritz values) of $A$.

In the single-vector case $b=1$, Paige's classical finite-precision analysis establishes rigorous componentwise bounds on the loss of local orthogonality, the size of the residual $A\hat{X}_k - \hat{X}_{k-1}\hat{B}_{k-1}^T - \hat{X}_k \hat{M}_k - \hat{X}_{k+1}\hat{B}_k$, the deviation $\hat{X}_k^T \hat{X}_k - I$, and the consequences for Ritz value behavior, all in terms of the unit roundoff $\varepsilon$, the dimensions, and $\|A\|$.

Problem. Develop an analogue of Paige's finite-precision analysis for the block Lanczos algorithm. Specifically, under either (a) explicit, quantitative assumptions on the accuracy of the intra-block orthogonalization scheme used to produce each $\hat{X}_{k+1}$ and $\hat{B}_k$ (e.g.\ bounds of the form $\|\hat{X}_{k+1}^T \hat{X}_{k+1} - I\| \le c\,\varepsilon$ and $\|\hat{X}_{k+1}\hat{B}_k - W_k\| \le c\,\varepsilon\|W_k\|$ for the input $W_k$), or (b) the choice of a specific standard orthogonalization scheme (such as Householder QR or twice-modified Gram–Schmidt), prove rigorous bounds in terms of $\varepsilon$, $b$, $k$, $n$, and $\|A\|$ on:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the residual of the block three-term recurrence;
\item the loss of orthogonality $\|\hat{X}_j^T \hat{X}_k\|$ both within blocks and between blocks;
\item the size and structure of the perturbations needed so that the computed $T_k$ may be interpreted as arising from an exact block Lanczos process applied to a nearby larger symmetric matrix; and
\item the implications for the accuracy of the computed Ritz values as approximations to eigenvalues of $A$.
\end{enumerate}
